# EL-2 — a second proxy nonce, from a different proxy on a different session.
#
# Provenance (LP-1, `Tests/FIXTURES.md`): a second capture of the maintainer's
# own EchoLink session, 2026-08-12, against a *different* public proxy than the
# one in `live-proxy-login-in.hex`. Cited by SHA-256, never by path: this
# capture contains the entire EchoLink directory — thousands of other
# operators' callsigns and addresses — so no type 0x02 frame may ever be cut
# from it, and only this one unframed inbound line is taken.
#
# Why a second nonce is worth a fixture of its own: EL-5's digest construction
# is `MD5(password ‖ nonce-as-8-ASCII-characters)`, and one (nonce, digest)
# pair can be reproduced by coincidence in a large enough candidate search.
# Two pairs, from two proxies, cannot. The digests themselves are pinned in the
# EL-5 tests rather than here — they are derived entirely from public inputs
# (the literal password "PUBLIC" and a nonce the proxy sent in clear), so they
# are evidence, not a secret, but they belong beside the assertion that uses
# them.
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py <capture> --transport tcp --stream 0 --dir in --range 0:0
# Capture SHA-256: 845451742f16dac6a1d14380fc23fa890db864e1c7ef852f63676b7a02a02483
#
# Each line is one EchoLink proxy frame verbatim, reassembled from the
# TCP stream: the 9-byte header — type(1), peer IPv4(4), length(4,
# LITTLE-endian) — and its payload. `[n]` is the frame's index in
# arrival order across both directions, so a gap in the indices is a
# frame deliberately left out.

# [0] 0.000s <== LOGIN        nonce 45801e6e      8B
3435383031653665
